          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#lorentzian_manifold Lorentzian manifold
          %p
            A
            %em Lorentzian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \eta)
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d+1)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            of signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [+--\cdots -]
            (but note that the complementary choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-++\cdots +]
            is also used in the literature). This equips the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_x X
            at every point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            canonically with the structure of a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d+1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T_x X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/timelike"} timelike
            ,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            , if their norm-square
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_x(v,v)
            is positive, zero or negative, respectively. See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+structure"} causal structure
          %h3#causal_structure Causal structure
          %p
            A
            %strong time orientation
            on a Lorentzian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a smooth (or, depending on the author, continuous)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu \in \Gamma(T X)
            such that at all points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            the vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_x
            is timelike. In general, a Lorentzian manifold does not have globally defined timelike continuous vector fields. Sometimes only Lorentzian manifolds admitting a time orientation are also called
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            Given a time orientation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T_x X
            is
            %strong future directed
            if it is timelike or light-like and its inner product with the time orientation vector at that point is positive,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_x(\nu,v_x) \gt 0
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            itself is smooth, it follows that it is future directed with respect to itself at every point.
          %p
            A smooth curve in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            is called a
            %strong timelike curve
            or a
            %strong lightlike curve
            or a
            %strong spacelike curve
            or a
            %strong future-directed curve
            precisely if all of its tangent vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\gamma_* \partial_s) \in T_{\gamma(s)} X
            are.
          %p
            We say that a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
            %strong lies in the future
            of a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = x
            or if there exists a future-directed curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(0) = x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(1) = y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %strong lies in the past
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %h4#notions_of_causality Notions of causality
          %p
            We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mu)
            has
            %strong closed timelike curves
            (closed future-directed curves) if there exists a non-constant timelike (future-dierected) curve starting and ending at some point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %strong chronological
            = succeed "." do
              %strong causal
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a time orientated spacetime L, the
              %strong chronological future
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^+(p)
              of a point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in L
              is the set of events that can be reached by a future directed timelike curve starting from p:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mtext there exists a future directed timelike curve
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mtext with
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mi and
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    I^+(p) := \{ q \in L | \text{there exists a future directed timelike curve} \lambda(t) \text{with} \lambda(0) = p and \lambda(1)=q \}
            %p
              The
              %strong causal future
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^+(p)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is defined in the same way with future directed timelike replaced by future directed causal aka non-spacelike.
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A subset S of a time orientated spacetime L is said to be
              %strong achronal
              if no two points in S can be connected by a future directed timelike curve, i.e. for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p, q \in L
              we have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∉
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \notin I^+(p)
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong boundary of chronological future
              Let L be a time orientated spacetime and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^+(S)
              is either empty or an achronal, three-dimensional, embedded,
              = succeed "-submanifold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^0
          %p This is theorem 8.1.3 in the book of Wald.
          %p
            Examples of non-chronological Lorentzian manifolds are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+space"} anti de Sitter space
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerr+spacetime"} Kerr spacetime
          %p
            While the former is more of a theoretical interest due to the maximality of the symmetry group, the latter is usually seen as a solution with relevance to actual
            = succeed "," do
              %em physics
          %p
            Note that the property of being chronological is not strong enough to enforce causality as understood in everyday life: Even if there are no
            %em closed
            future-directed curves, there still may be e.g. nonclosed
            %em ergodic
            future-directed curves (they come close to every point they already passed in the “past”). An often used stronger condition that models the everyday notion of causality is that the manifold has to be
            %span.newWikiWord
              globally hyperbolic
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/globally+hyperbolic+manifold"} ?
            (
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globally_hyperbolic"}> Wikipedia
            ), which, as already mentioned, excludes certain solutions modelling e.g. black holes.
          %h4#being_causal_means_being_a_poset Being causal means being a poset
          %p
            Precisely if the Lorentzian space is causal in that there are
            %em no
            closed future-directed curves is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \leq y) \Leftrightarrow
              “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is in the future of
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with at most a single morphism between any two objects:
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to y
            is a pair of points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in the future of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is the reflexivity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq x
          %p
            The
            %em anti-symmetry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \leq y \leq x) \Rightarrow (x = y)
            is precisely the absence of closed future-directed curves in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Conversely, from just knowing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a smooth manifold and knowing this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong light cone structure
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mu)
          %p One can see
          %blockquote
            %p (…reference…)
          %p
            that the pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            may be reconstructed from the lightcone structure and the
            %span.newWikiWord
              volume density
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/volume+density"} ?
            that it induces. In this sense a Lorentzian manifold without future-directed curves is equivalently a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            equipped with a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            on its space of objects.
          %h3#generalized_smooth_lorentzian_spaces Generalized smooth Lorentzian spaces
          %p …
          %blockquote
            %p
              A
              %strong smooth Lorentzian space
              is supposed to be like a Lorentzian manifold, but whose underlying space is not necessarily a smooth manifold, but a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            %p
              So this is “something like” a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
              to a category of measure spaces, or a
              = succeed "-valued" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
              on something like
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              or the like.
          %p …
          %h2#construction_on_a_smooth_lorentzian_space Construction on a smooth Lorentzian space
          %h3#causal_subsets Causal subsets
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mu,\nu)
            be a time-oriented Lorentzian space regarded as a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
          %p
            A
            %strong causal subset
            of a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is one of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/under-over+category"} under-over categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \downarrow X \downarrow y
            for a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in X
            of points in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Its objects form the collection of all points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in X
            that are both in the future of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as well as in the past of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %h3#PathnCategory
            The path
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            To every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is associated its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            is associated a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                path
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} fundamental (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            A causal Lorentzian manifold may naturally be regarded as a smooth category (a smooth
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            = succeed "-category." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (2,1)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            of a plain manifold and is akin to the path 2-groupoid of paths in an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
          %p To put this construction into context, we therefore first recall the story for paths in an orbifold.
          %h4#prelude_the_path_2groupoid_of_an_orbifold Prelude: the path 2-groupoid of an orbifold
          %p
            To an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is associated its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            and its smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          %p
            This construction generalizes from paths in plain spaces, to paths in spaces that are themselves smooth groupoids: notably to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Given an orbifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with space of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            and space of morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            which looks as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
                are the points of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            %li
              %p
                the morphisms of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
                are formal composites of two types of morphisms
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    the smooth paths
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi P
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X_0)
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi γ
                          %mo :
                          %mi x
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : x \to y
                %li
                  %p
                    the original morphisms of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi x
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to g\cdot x
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X_0
                    to the point
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\cdot x
                    that it is
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
                    to under the orbifold action.
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
                are paths in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X_1)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi x
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi γ
                            %mtd
                              %mi y
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⇙
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi g
                              %mo ⋅
                              %mi x
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi g
                                  %mo ⋅
                                  %mi γ
                            %mtd
                              %mi g
                              %mo ⋅
                              %mi y
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      x &amp;\stackrel{\gamma}{\to}&amp; y
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow
                      \\\
                      g\cdot x &amp;\underset{g\cdot \gamma}{\to}&amp; g \cdot y
                      }
                      \\,.
              %p
                This is a path
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\gamma, g\cdot \gamma)
                of pairs of points that are related under the orbifold action.
          %p
            The path 2-groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            of the orbifold encodes the correct notion of trajectories in the orbifold: such a trajectory may proceed along smooth paths, and intermittently it may jump between the “orbifold sectors”. Notably an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            on a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            may be given by a smooth path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi g
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to g x
            that does not come back to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            but just to one of its mirror-images, composed with the jump-morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g x \to x
            back to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em twisted sectors
            of loop configurations.
          %p
            A detailed description of the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            of paths in a smooth 2-groupoid may be found in
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/arxiv/pdf/0808/0808.1923v1.pdf#page=19"} section 2.1
            of
          %ul
            %li
              U.S.,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Konrad Waldorf
              %em Connections on nonabelian gerbes and their holonomy
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1923"}> arXiv:0808.1923
              )
          %p
            There the groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is taken to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+nerve"} Cech groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
          %p
            It is immediate how this construction generalizes when the smooth groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is replaced by a smooth category. This we turn to now.
          %h4#the_path_category_of_a_lorentzian_space_2
            The path
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
          %p
            Now we discuss the same as above, where now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = (X_1 \stackrel{\to}{\to} X_0)
            is not a smooth groupoid, but a smooth category, notably the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            determined by a smooth Lorentzian space.
          %p
            So let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a smooth causal Lorentzian manifold, regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            is the underlying manifold and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \subset X_0 \times X_0
            is the collection of pairs of points with one in the future of the other.
          %p
            We equip this with the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} category internal
            to
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : U \to X_0
            of the space of objects is a
            %em spacelike
            smooth map
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            of every tangent vector of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi CartSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in CartSp
            yields a spacelike vector in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
          %p
            Analogously, we declare a plot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi  : U \to X_1
            to be a pair of plots into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            such that pointwise this assigns a point and one point in its future.
          %p
            From now on, by abuse of notation, by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we shall mean this category internal to diffeological spaces, regarded as a category-valued presheaf on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
          %p
            Then the path
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1(X)
            is defined as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                its space of objects is again the diffeological space
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            %li
              %p the elements of its space of morphisms are generated from
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    morphisms
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi γ
                          %mo :
                          %mi x
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : x \to y
                    given by reparameterization or thin-hoimotopy classes of smooth spacelike curves
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi γ
                            %mo :
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo →
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X_0
                %li
                  %p
                    morphisms of the form
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to y
                    for every
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
                    in the causal structure of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                There is an evident diffeology on this space (a quotient of a disjoint union of product diffeologies). This defines the diffeological space
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P_1(X))
            %li
              %p the elements of its space of 2-morphisms are generated from 2-morphisms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↗
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                    %msub
                                      %mi γ
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi x
                                %mn 1
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi y
                                %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⇙
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi y
                                %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↘
                              %msub
                                %mo ↗
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                    %msub
                                      %mi γ
                                      %mn 2
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      \&amp; \nearrow \searrow^{\mathrlap{[\gamma_1]}}
                      \\\
                      x_1  &amp;&amp; y_1
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow
                      \\\
                      x_2  &amp;&amp; y_2
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \searrow \nearrow_{\mathrlap{[\gamma_2]}}
                      }
                      \\;\;\;\;
                      \\forall t \in [0,1] : \gamma_1(t) \leq \gamma_2(t)
              %p
                given from classes of smooth paths in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
              %p There is an evident diffeology and evident composition operations on this. Notice that the generating 2-cells are 2-isomorphisms, but that their source and target morphisms are not generally invertible.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+geometry"} Lorentzian geometry
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+Lorentzian+manifold"} globally hyperbolic Lorentzian manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conal+manifold"} conal manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+orbifold"} Lorentzian orbifold
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hendrik+Lorentz"} Hendrik Lorentz
          %p A classic reference for general relativity is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"}> Robert Wald
              ,
              %em General Relativity
          %p
            A textbook dedicated to the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} diffential geometric
            aspects Lorentzian manifolds is
          %ul
            %li
              John K. Beem; Paul E. Ehrlich, ; Kevin L. Easley,
              %em Global Lorentzian geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0846.53001&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
              )
          %p A classical influential text on the nature of Lorentzian space is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Rindler"} Wolfgang Rindler
              = succeed "," do
                %em Spinors and space time
          %p
            On Lorentz structure as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
          %ul
            %li
              Leandro A. Lichtenfelz,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Piccione"} Paolo Piccione
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Isometry Group of Lorentz Manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %em Recent Trends in Lorentzian Geometry
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-4897-6_12"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4614-4897-6_12
          %p
            The relation between causality and
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+set"} causal set
          %ul
            %li
              Ettore Minguzzi,
              %em Time and Causality in General Relativity
              , talk notes, Ponta Delgada, July 2009 (
              %a{:href => "http://fqxi.org/data/documents/Minguzzi%20%20Azores%20Talk.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            More details are discussed in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+theory"} domain theory
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                Keye Martin
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Keye+Martin"} ?
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prakash+Panangaden"} Prakash Panangaden
              %a{:href => "http://www.springerlink.com/content/m3x4h1l6g64mu753/"}
              %p Domain Theory and the Causal Structure of Space-Time&lt;/a&gt;
          %p Some vaguely related blog discussion is at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/10/the_nature_of_time.html#c019330"} The Nature of Time
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/03/canonical_measures_on_configur_1.html"} Canonical Measures on Configuration Spaces
        .revisedby
